menu "Device protocol primitives"

config PROTO_ANKER
    bool "Anker frame/TLV codec and session crypto"
    default y
    help
      The stable, byte-level half of the Anker protocol: the FF09 frame header
      and its XOR checksum, fragment reassembly, both TLV dialects, and the
      AES/GCM/ECDH the session cipher needs.

      This is device-named C, which looks like it contradicts the rule that
      device knowledge stays out of the firmware. It does not. The rule
      protects the generic Bluetooth core -- src/bt, src/ops, src/api,
      src/lua -- so the gateway stays useful for any peripheral. A byte codec
      that has been stable across three independent implementations is a
      primitive, not a policy. The opcodes, field dictionaries, handshake and
      keystream constants live in the Anker PEX module (and in lua/profiles,
      which is the behavior spec that module was ported from).

      Turn this off for a build that will never talk to Anker hardware and
      reclaim the flash. The Lua bindings disappear with it.

config PROTO_ANKER_CRYPTO
    bool "Session crypto (P-256 ECDH, AES, GCM)"
    depends on PROTO_ANKER
    default y
    help
      ECDH runs on the uECC that BTstack already links for LE Secure
      Connections, so it costs nothing extra; AES comes from a vendored
      tiny-AES-c at about 3 KB, and GCM is CTR over that plus a GHASH. mbedTLS
      would have done all of it and cost roughly 80 KB, because the SDK builds
      it for picotool rather than for the firmware.

      Without this only the frame and TLV codecs are built, which is enough to
      decode a plaintext capture but not to hold a session.

endmenu
